.TH TCP 5N UniSoft
.SH NAME
tcp \- Internet Transmission Control Protocol
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.sp
.B "struct sockproto proto = { PF_INET, IPPROTO_TCP };
.B socket(SOCK_STREAM, &proto, address, options);
.B struct sockaddr_in \(**address; int options;
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The 
.SM 
.I TCP\^
protocol provides reliable, flow-controlled, two-way
transmission of data.  It is a byte-stream protocol used to
support the 
.SM SOCK_STREAM 
abstraction.  
.SM 
.I TCP\^
uses the standard
Internet address format and, in addition, provides a per-host
collection of "port addresses".  Thus, each address is composed
of an Internet address specifying the host and network, with
a specific 
.SM 
.I TCP\^
port on the host identifying the peer entity.
.PP
Sockets utilizing the 
.SM 
.I TCP\^ 
protocol are either "active" or
"passive".  Active sockets initiate connections to passive
sockets.  By default 
.SM 
.I TCP\^
sockets are created active; to create a
passive socket the 
.SM SO_ACCEPTCONN
option must be supplied.  Only
passive sockets may use the 
.IR accept\^ (2N)
call to accept incoming connections.  Only active sockets may
use the
.IR connect\^ (2N)
call to initiate connections.
.PP
Passive sockets may "underspecify" their location to match
incoming connection requests from multiple networks.  This
technique, termed "wildcard addressing", allows a single
server to provide service to clients on multiple networks.
To create a socket which listens on all networks, the Internet
address
.SM
.I INADDR_ANY
is specified.  The 
.SM 
.I TCP\^
port may still be specified
at this time; if the port is not specified, the system will assign one.
Once a connection has been established the socket's address is
fixed by the peer entity's location.   The address assigned the
socket is the address associated with the network interface
through which packets are being transmitted and received.  Normally
this address corresponds to the peer entity's network.
.SH OPTIONS
The 
.SM 
.I TCP\^
implementation supports two non-standard features:
"keep-alives" and "true out-of-band" data transmission.
.PP
Keep-alives are a mechanism used to check if a peer entity
is still functional.  This is implemented by periodically
"polling" the remote machine if the connection has been
idle.  The current implementation transmits keep-alive
packets on a connection which has been idle for longer than
1 minute.  If, despite the keep-alive packets, no response
has been seen within 4 minutes, the connection is aborted.
This mechanism applies only to connection in an "established"
state; if a connection is idle for 1 minute but not yet
established, it is simply aborted.  The keep-alive mechanism
is enabled by creating a socket with the 
.SM SO_KEEPALIVE
option.
.RB [\| N.B. : 
.SM 
.I TCP\^
implementations which do not closely follow the 
.SM 
.I TCP\^
specification may not respond to keep-alive messages,
causing connections to be closed without reason; in this case
keep-alives should not be used\|]
.PP
In order to transmit "true" out-of-band data, the 
.SM SO_TRUEOOB
option may be specified.  This facility requires cooperation
by the peer to function properly; this is negotiated through
.SM 
.I TCP\^ 
options at the time a connection is established.  When
this mechanism is used, one byte of data may be sent as an urgent,
high-priority message to the peer.  This data utilizes a separate,
out-of-band data sequence space and is not subject to the normal
flow control mechanisms imposed by 
.SM 
.IR TCP\*S .
In addition, the data
stream is also marked
to indicate the point at which the out-of-band data was sent.
A process may send out-of-band data with the 
.SM SIOCSENDOOB
call,
.IP
ioctl(fd, SIOCSENDOOB, &data);
.PP
and receive out-of-band data with the 
.SM SIOCRCVOOB
call,
.IP
ioctl(fd, SIOCRCVOOB, &data);
.PP
To find out if the read pointer is at the mark in the data
stream, the 
.SM SIOCATMARK
call may be used,
.IP
ioctl(fd, SIOCATMARK, &yesno);
.PP
The variable
.I yesno\^
will be a 1 if the read pointer currently points at the mark,
and 0 otherwise.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.SM EISCONN
when trying to establish a connection on a socket which
already has one;
.PP
.SM ENOBUFS
when the system runs out of memory for
an internal data structure;
.PP
.SM ETIMEDOUT
when a connection was dropped
due to excessive retransmissions;
.PP
.SM ECONNRESET
when the remote peer
forces the connection to be closed;
.PP
.SM ECONNREFUSED
when the remote
peer actively refuses connection establishment (usually because
no process is listening to the port);
.PP
.SM EADDRINUSE
when an attempt
is made to create a socket with a port which has already been
allocated;
.PP
.SM EADDRNOTAVAIL
when an attempt is made to create a 
socket with a network address for which no network interface
exists.
.SH SEE ALSO
inet(5N), net(5N). 
.SH BUGS
Value added "features" such as "keep-alives"
and "true" out-of-band are experimental and not
part of the protocol standard.
.\"N	 @(#)tcp.5N	 5.2 of 7/12/84
